\section{Overview}

The Berzerk hardware fires 10 interrupts per frame: 2 IRQs and 8 NMIs at
specific scanline positions.  The game code depends on these interrupts for
timing, input polling, and sound updates.

Rather than using host-side timers (as MAME does with screen.time\_until\_pos),
we use cycle-count scheduling.  Each frame is approximately 41,918 Z80
T-states (262 scanlines $\times$ 320 pixels $\div$ 2 pixels/T-state).  We
pre-compute a sorted table of 10 events with their cycle offsets, then call
[[cpu.execute()]] in chunks, firing the appropriate interrupt at each
boundary.

\subsection{Scanline Timing}

\begin{itemize}
\item Pixel clock: 5.000~MHz
\item Pixels per line: 320
\item Lines per frame: 262
\item CPU clock: 2.500~MHz (pixel clock / 2)
\item T-states per scanline: $320 / 2 = 160$
\item T-states per frame: $262 \times 160 = 41{,}920$
\end{itemize}

The V-counter counts scanlines from 0 to 261.  Visible lines are 32--255
(V-counter values 0x20--0xFF with V256=0).  Lines 256--261 and 0--31 are
the vblank region (V256=1 for lines $\ge$256, but the counter wraps).

\subsection{Interrupt Positions}

The 10 events, sorted by cycle offset:

\begin{verbatim}
Type  V-counter  V256  Scanline  Cycle Offset
NMI   0x30       0     48        7,680
NMI   0x50       0     80        12,800
NMI   0x70       0     112       17,920
IRQ   0x80       0     128       20,480
NMI   0x90       0     144       23,040
NMI   0xB0       0     176       28,160
NMI   0xD0       0     208       33,280
NMI   0xF0       0     240       38,400
NMI   0xF0       1     ~256      40,960
IRQ   0xDA       1     ~vblank   41,600
\end{verbatim}

For V256=0 events, the scanline number equals the V-counter value.  For
V256=1 events, the scanline is approximately 256 + (V-counter - 0xE8),
though the exact mapping depends on the sync chain counter conversion.  We
use fixed cycle offsets derived from MAME's timer scheduling.

